{
  "index": 49,
  "text": "Farag et al. analyzed 32 tumor studies of the HALP score, concluding that it independently predicts poor prognostic outcomes and tumor grading or differentiation [30]. Median HALP scores varied across tumor subtypes (overall 31.2), being higher in gastric cancer (56.8) and adenocarcinoma (44.56) and lower in colorectal (26.5) and bladder (22.2). Although no standardized threshold is valid for all cancers, low HALP scores were statistically significant for poor outcome in most intergroup comparisons [6, 31\u201336]. In our study, the median HALP score was 23.35, illustrating the score\u2019s heterogeneity; it was also significantly lower in non-survivors at 30-day and 1-year mortality.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 50,
  "text": "The relationship between HALP score and mortality has been studied in various medical contexts. Tian et al. [37] found a correlation between HALP scores and poor outcomes in acute ischemic stroke patients. Peng et al. [34] reported a strong correlation between HALP scores and cancer-specific survival in renal cell carcinoma patients, while Ruiz et al. [38] did not find a significant association between HALP scores and disease-free survival in colon cancer patients. Njoku et al. [39] also did not find a correlation between HALP scores and overall, cancer-specific, or recurrence-free survival in endometrial cancer patients. In addition, HALP has also shown remarkable prognostic ability in predicting survival and as a disease severity index in some non-cancer studies [40, 41]. In our study, the 1-year mortality risk of patients with a HALP score < 17.975 was approximately 12 times that of patients with a HALP score > 17.975.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

